From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Fri, 23 Sep 1994 12:25:11 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-429] What Happens First?

Robin Hanson writes:
> Nick clearly sees long meat lifespans and reproducing comet eaters 
> and AI as much easier than I do, relative [to] uploading for
> example.  Care to explain why those look easy?

Sufficient simulation of a human personality probably has to include pre- 
and post-synaptic chemical reactions, including those in the peripherary 
nervous system.  It also must simulate the hormonal system, and may 
have to include simulation of structures like microtubules.  Given 
the current state of the art in molecular modeling software this would 
likely be computationally infeasible, even two centuries from now
at current rates of computational improvement.  Thus uploading 
relies on either some fundamental advances in molecular modelling, 
or on creating a sufficiently accurate high-level abstraction of 
neural and hormonal function, or perhaps just several centuries of 
improvement in computational performance at the current rate.  
Furthermore, the accurate transcription of molecules from the 
human anatomy must occur (eg via destructive STM of a frozen 
brain, which means solving the protein folding problem as frozen 
proteins have lost their natural folds).  Finally, there is 
the problem of testing an upload system to determine whether the 
upload has been sufficiently accurate.  All of these are unsolved 
problems, in my guestimation tougher than advances in our understanding 
of and ability to manipulate metabolsims, sufficient for life extension 
and a self-reproducing, comet-eating greenhouse; also tougher than
diamondoid nanotech.

A concrete point of comparison: the task of designing proteins 
to fold a specific way, the "reverse protein folding problem", 
is deemed by Drexler and many protein scientists to be easier 
than simulating the folding of naturally occuring proteins.
Designing specific proteins (and metabolisms based around their 
enzymatic activity), using easier simulations that, furthermore, 
need to be peformed only a few times offline, will be far easier 
than real time simulation of natural proteins and their relationship 
to biological functions in the nervous system and elsewhere.

> How do comet eaters come so far before reproducing assemblers?

I judged comet eaters to come at about the same time as nanotech
factories, and a bit (3 points) before fully reproducing assemblers.  
(3 points might be 9-90 years, depending on how you judge rates of 
change).

I will probably be feasible to make self-reproducing vacuum greenhouses
with advanced biotech.  Organisms can survive in a wide variety of 
hostile environments -- spiders have survived substantial periods 
in vacuum, Dead Sea critters maintain water/ion concentrations 
substantially higher than their environment, lichens grow
on sun-warmed Antartic rocks in weather otherwise well below
freezing, etc.  The building blocks for vacuum resistance appear
to be available.  The general idea of the comet eater is 
a shield of dead fiber, with vacuum-hardy critters as
gatekeepers; the more fragile but productive critters grow
inside in a low pressue CO2-rich atmosphere.  This is easier
if the creatures can chemosynthesize free radicals frozen
in the comet.  Photosynthesis on an insulated sun warmed surface, 
underneath a thin protective film, may also be sufficient.

Also, it might be much easer to build an assembler that starts 
with high-level building blocks produced by biotech, rather than 
with primitive molecules.  Such "half assemblers" could be part 
of the self-reproducing greenhouse system, performing tasks
like processing fiber, moving it from the interior to the exterior, 
coating it with resin, etc.   Assuming I would need half assemblers, 
I make comet eaters contemporaneous with nanotech factories,
which in turn come before assemblers because they are both
easier and (due to comparative advantage of specialization)
more lucrative than assemblers.

Finally, advanced biotech is on a direct tooling path from 
today's technology, while nanotechnology (and even moreso, uploading) 
require a very different industrial ecology.  Even if nanotech and 
uploading were slightly more feasible in theory, in practice we'd get
the biotech stuff first because that's where industry and
academia is starting from today.  Orders of magnitude more
students are being trained in biotech than in nanotech; 
few if any are being trained to do anything sufficiently
close to uploading.

