From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Fri, 23 Sep 1994 13:01:20 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-431] Nanotech

Mike Price: 
> Will reproducing assemblers come at all?  Wee reproductive beasties may,
> but I've never seen a convincing argument for how they will assemble
> anything.

Drexler's argument is that his diamondoid robots are "universal
constructors".  They can construct any structure that can be 
feasibly constructed from diamondoid, in particular
copies of themselves.  By using such a standard building block
he greatly reduces the number of operations that needs to be 
performed: just a few reactions like hydrogen extraction to build 
up a wide variety of diamondoid structures.  However, there are enough 
details left out (eg, the specific reactions needed to assemble the 
reactive tips themselves, the specific reactions needed to assemble some 
of the small bearings and Kaehler brackets, etc.) that I agree that 
Drexler hasn't proved closure.  He has come a lot closer than anybody 
else, certainly much closer than that abominable 1980 NASA study.

Also, some of the theoretical assumptions behind the bearing
movements have been questioned, especially in regards to what
happens when the parts start and stop moving.  This regime
deserves more in depth theoretical and experimental investigation.
This is not in any way to slight the work done so far by Drexler,
Merkle, et. al. which is nothing short of spectacular.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

