a case of sour grapes, since ExI was written up in both Wired and the
Examiner in the same week, and the best you could manage was "Mondo 2000"?

There's a difference between objecting to people saying true but
unflattering things about you (which Rheingold did), and objecting to
saying inaccurate, unflattering things (which Rheingold also did).  The
first kind of objection is vanity; the second is righteous indignation.
Let's look at some of the inaccuracies Rheingold included in his "neutral"
article:

>EXTROPIANS AND THE WILD BLUE YONDER
>
>Extropians are like other humans, except the word "impossible"
>means "possible at some time in the future" in their vocabulary.

This is a gratuitous insult.  Extropians are more aware of real physical
limits (the inability of nanotech to implement nucleosynthesis, for
example) than most readers of the Examiner, I'll bet.  (And I'll undertake
a close reading of glaring inaccuracies in Charles' "Mondo" profile if
anyone's interested.)  Rheingold is unable to distinguish between those
more optimistic than he is, and those who've got what Tim May calls
"boundless optimism".  Extropians are among the former.

>To other humans, physical death is the end of all possibilities.
>To Extropians, many of whom have arranged to have their brains
>preserved in liquid nitrogen after death, it's just a matter of
>time before a technology for recovering life from dead tissue comes
>along.

This is a monstrous oversimplification that ignores the problems of proper
preparation and suspension techniques for preserving structural
information.  Stated this stupidly, it makes it sound as though cryonicists
expect a straight freeze to be sufficient.  And calling a suspendee "dead
tissue" is begging the question.

>The term "Luddite" is used pejoratively to describe people who
>question technological progress.  I propose using the name
>"hubrists" to describe people such as the Extropians, who believe
