As has already been pointed out, political, not technological, hubris
caused the Chernobyl disaster (and the 10^8 other ecological crises
afflicting the XSSR).  The unparalleled safety records of the U.S. Navy's
nuclear submarines demonstrates that one need not aspire to godhead to
operate a nuke safely.

>Extropians [...] argue that since human
>minds have harnessed the force that fuels the stars, who is to say
>that we will never crack the immortality barrier, build microscopic
>machines or migrate into space?

Baldly stated, Rheingold is imputing to us the syllogism, "Somebody once
said X is impossible; X later happened; therefore anything that anyone says
is impossible will someday happen."  Shouldn't we be a little upset at this
accusation?

>Other articles
>cover the possibility of uploading minds to computers and the
>prospects for teleportation technology.

Teleportation?  I've got a lovely MST3K "Satellite of Love" pin for anyone
who can find an article about physical teleportation in Issue #14.  (What
_is_ Rheingold referring to here, anyway?  The inertialess motion of
uploaded minds in a Utility Fog?  That's the only teleportation-like topic
I can find anywhere in Extropy.)

>It's a big universe, and we know very little about it.  Are we sure
>it's wise to download our minds into self-reproducing robots and
>blast off into outer space, just because we might have the
>technical capability?

How the hell are we going to find out more about this "big universe" unless
we do exactly that?  It's not going to come to us, folks.

Charles, I suggest you lurk a while longer (and keep your sour grapes to
yourself), before making an idiot of yourself again in this fashion.

