	I'm re-posting something I sent to another list some
weeks ago. It's fairly relevant to what has been discussed
lately on Anarchy-list. Although my focus below is on the
concept of Worker's Militia, please substitute the concept of
armed political or social revolution of any kind.
	I wish to strongly state that ANY atempt at a
political revolution will fail: it will be corrupted and
become a totalitarian dictatorship by a vanguard, or it will be
bought off by the capitalists, or it will be crushed by force of
arms. My fondest wish is that the Left would completely
reject the idea of a political/social revolution. It just
won't work in the modern world. Economic revolution is
a realistic alternative -- and computer technology (data
encryption, digital cash) make a powerful economic revolution
(one that ignores the existence of the state) a real alternative.
I think the road to freedom lies before us -- but many are
walking down the path that led to Mexico in 1914, Ukraine 1917,
Spain 1936, etc. Get a clue! Try something else!
	Social revolution occurs _with_ an economic revolution.
Social revolution does _not_ occur with a political revolution,
because the political revolution will not survive the efforts
of the State.

		*************************

        Someone once said that the fascists would have dealt
with Gandhi by simply shooting him before he had a chance
to have an impact on his society. Probably. But I still would 
have to say that civil disobedience and non-violence can go a 
long way to short-circuit the power of the state.
        China isn't free: never has been. Tiannamen (sp?) Square
can occur in a society like that. But in America? If the National
Guard had simply massacred 100's of people in the post-Rodney 
King riot?? I don't think the government could survive such a
travesty. My point is that the US government is not likely
to act in a terribly violent manner UNLESS they are faced with
an armed insurrection (a Worker's Militia). In such an event,
the government will be willing to kill a lot of people.
Remember Waco, Texas?? The mere presence of arms at the Branch 
Dravidian compound gave the US the pretext for a long siege 
and eventual destruction of some 80 men, women and children.

        Non-violence is likely to succeed in any society that
is used to a moderate amount of civility, liberty, and justice.
An armed insurrection cannot succeed against a large, powerful
army with sophisiticated weapons. By arming yourself you
give your opponent "permission" to use lethal force.

        I should also say that I favor Economic Revolution, not
political revolution. The people who line the barriciades are
usually just as bad as the bureaucrats they oppose. Often worse.

        In an economic revolution, the power would slowly bleed
from the state. No confrontation would be necessary. If people
ignore the state as much as possible and exist without it's
"assistance" and consciously disobey it, it probably will die
fairly quietly. 
	The establishment of any sort of Worker's Militia is 
apt to result in a Warsaw Ghetto holocaust. At _best_, the 
state might "shoot itself in the foot" by revealing its 
barbaric nature and killing a lot of citizens. This _might_ 
convince more people to resist the authority of the state, or
it might convince everyone to very quietly do what the
government says. For the most part, the militia would be a 
bold (and hopeless) gesture. Why provide a target?

        If a political "experiment" were attempted in a
particular section of the US, then the State might sweep in
and try to crush it. (Like the Paris Commune.) But, if a
decentralized economic "experiment" were attempted 
throughout the territory of the US, the State would not know 
where to strike. Have you seen statisitics on the number 
of self-employed people in the US? The number of people 
working out of their homes? These people already have one 
foot outside the system. If they choose to, they can bring 
government to it's knees simply by pretending that the 
government doesn't exist. I see the general strike as a 
last ditch weapon, but I think it would have a remarkable 
effect. If 80 million Americans stopped paying taxes in 1995,
what on earth would the government do? If the government had
no access to the underground economy, it could not garnish 
people's paychecks, and it would be quite helpless.

        I favor emphasis on economic self-dependence: digital
cash, data encryption and civil disobedience. The state _will_
wither away. Political revolution simply plays into the
strength of the State. Sap their strength by ignoring them!
        Create systems and organizations that exist without
"assistance" from the State. Then ignore the old systems
and just use the new systems.
        How can the US government (which claims to be free
and democratic) try to stop such activity without giving the
game away and revealing itself as a totalitarian monster?
I bet that would "raise the consciousness" of a whole lot
of the "unconverted" citizens! I think government would lose
more than it gained by trying to crush such new,
decentralized, grass-roots systems. Government suppression
would have to be as widespread and decentralized as the
worker's movement itself. Such a decentralized effort does NOT
serve the needs of the aggressor, but only helps the defense
of the seccessionists. 
	Organize worker's militias and you give the Central
State a chance to smash the center of your strength. But, 
move toward a decentralized non-governmental system and you 
force the Central State to pull itself apart trying to 
attack the enemies all around it. Centralized rebels CANNOT 
win against a strong centralized state. And a centralized
State CANNOT win against decentralized economic rebels.

